#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""eos-power — off/on/cycle the EOS 2000D's 12V dummy-battery feed.

The 12V rail is the ONLY reset that clears a Class-B firmware wedge (0x2019
PTP Device Busy / -110); the camera power switch does NOT, because the
dummy-battery PSU holds the USB device alive across a switch flip (astro-canon
STATE, Reset ladder). A **Matter smart plug** switches mains to the DR-E10
dummy-battery adapter, so a wedge can be cleared remotely instead of by a human
pulling the plug. (This is strand option (b): reuse a smart mains plug rather
than a GPIO relay in the 12V line — coarser, whole-adapter, but no new
hardware. Commissioned 2026-07-26; see home-automation STATE.)

  eos-power off      # cut mains to the DR-E10 adapter (12V rail down)
  eos-power on       # restore mains (rail up)
  eos-power cycle    # off, wait --secs, on  (the STATE-validated ~10s pull)
  eos-power status   # best-effort: is the camera enumerated on muppet?

HARDWARE — two backends, selected by PLUG_BACKEND:
  "zha"    (LIVE since 2026-08-12) SONOFF S60ZBTPG "Canon EOS Power", paired to
           ZHA on homepi, driven via the HA REST API. Replaced the Realwe after
           the Matter plugs proved unreliable. Entities are ZHA_SWITCH etc.
  "matter" (retired fallback) Realwe plug via the python-matter-server WS API,
           matched by MATTER_VENDOR. Kept so a swap back needs no code archaeology.

  PLUG_ON_POWERS_CAMERA : True if plug ON = mains present = camera powered
                          (normal wiring; the plug feeds the adapter directly)

WHY THE Dev-NUMBER CHECK SURVIVED THE SWAP — AND WHY WATTS ARE NOT USED AT ALL.
The S60ZBTPG meters wattage, and the obvious idea is to verify a power cut by
watching draw fall to ~0. It does not work here, for TWO independent reasons:

  1. THE EOS IS BELOW THE MEASUREMENT FLOOR (decisive; home-automation
     2026-08-12). The camera draws ~200 mW = ~0.0008 A at 240 V, against a
     0.01 A current step and 1 W power quantisation — about 1/12th of ONE step.
     The meter reads 0.0 W with the camera RUNNING NORMALLY, so 0 W means
     nothing: keying off it would false-pass a cycle that never happened or
     false-fail a healthy camera — the Sandstrom "plug lied" failure class
     reintroduced from the other end. Encouraging bench figures came from a
     20-40 W glue gun, not a camera load.
  2. The reporting is delta-driven and cannot be polled on demand (measured
     here before wiring): `homeassistant.update_entity` returns HTTP 200 but
     does NOT refresh the sample — last_updated AND last_reported both stayed
     frozen across two forced polls — and the voltage sensor sat at 238.02 V
     straight through a genuine off/on of its own relay.

So `cycle` reads NO watts. USB re-enumeration is the sole verification: it
proves the CAMERA lost power, not merely that a socket went dead. Revisit only
if a ~10 W resistive ballast is fitted in parallel on the switched side.
The plug remains fully trusted as a SWITCH — that half is verified good.

If the plug can't be reached, off/on/cycle print the failure and exit non-zero,
so the recovery protocol degrades safely (logs "power-cycle unavailable" rather
than silently doing nothing).

`cycle` VERIFIES the rail actually dropped by watching the camera's USB device
number (a real power cut forces re-enumeration with a NEW Dev). Smart plugs here
have proved outrageously unreliable, and a plug that ACKs the command without
switching mains is otherwise indistinguishable from an uncurable camera wedge.
Exit codes for `cycle`:
  0  cycled and verified (Dev changed) — or --no-verify
  2  plug unreachable / command failed (the reset never ran)
  3  PLUG LIED: ACKed but Dev unchanged — suspect the plug, not the camera
  4  camera never came back on the bus after the cycle
"""
import argparse, asyncio, json, re, subprocess, sys, time

# ---- hardware config --------------------------------------------------------
PLUG_BACKEND = "zha"        # "zha" (live, SONOFF S60ZBTPG) | "matter" (retired)

# -- ZHA backend (SONOFF S60ZBTPG "Canon EOS Power", HA REST API) --
# Entity ids, NOT a node number: ZHA re-pairing keeps the entity id, so a swap
# does not renumber the way Matter nodes did.
# NB there is a SECOND S60ZBTPG on this HA (sensor.sonoff_s60zbtpg_* = the TUMBLE
# DRYER, 107 kWh lifetime). Same model, different appliance — always drive the
# canon_eos_power_* entities, never the bare model-named ones. This is the same
# trap as the node-4 Sandstrom ghost that was also called "…Smart Plug".
HA_URL = "http://homepi.local:8123"
HA_TOKEN_SECRET = "/home-automation/ha-claude"   # via super/bin/secrets
ZHA_SWITCH = "switch.canon_eos_power"
ZHA_POWER = "sensor.canon_eos_power_power"       # W — second witness only
ZHA_VOLTAGE = "sensor.canon_eos_power_voltage"   # V

# -- Matter backend (retired Realwe plug) --
MATTER_WS = "ws://homepi.local:5580/ws"   # HA python-matter-server WS API
MATTER_NODE = 7                           # Realwe Innovation plug (node 7)
# Plugs get replaced and renumber on re-commissioning, so prefer matching the
# vendor name and fall back to MATTER_NODE. Set to None to match by id only.
# NB match the VENDOR (0/40/1), not the product: the retired Sandstrom ghost
# still on the fabric is also called "…Smart Plug".
MATTER_VENDOR = "Realwe"                  # 0/40/1 substring, case-insensitive
PLUG_ON_POWERS_CAMERA = True              # plug ON = mains up = camera powered
ONOFF_CLUSTER = 6                         # Matter On/Off cluster id
# 90s, NOT 10s. 10s is enough to make the camera re-enumerate with a new USB
# Dev number, which is why it looked sufficient (verified 2026-07-26) — but
# re-enumeration is NOT the same as clearing a Class-B firmware wedge. Measured
# 2026-08-09 on a real 0x2019/-110 wedge: cycles at 10s, 25s and 30s each gave a
# NEW Dev number and each left config writes still rejected; only a 90s pull
# cleared it. The dummy-battery adapter's caps hold the logic rail up long after
# the USB interface has dropped. So a changed Dev proves mains fell, not that
# the camera reset — and the historical "post-cycle: STILL WEDGED" results were
# probably just too-short pulls.
CYCLE_SECS = 90

def _configured():
    if PLUG_BACKEND == "zha":
        return bool(HA_URL and ZHA_SWITCH)
    return MATTER_WS is not None and MATTER_NODE is not None

# ---- ZHA backend (HA REST) --------------------------------------------------
_HA_TOKEN = None

def _ha_token():
    """Long-lived HA token from the house secrets manager (never hardcoded)."""
    global _HA_TOKEN
    if _HA_TOKEN is not None:
        return _HA_TOKEN
    # Resolve by ABSOLUTE PATH, never bare `secrets`: this runs from systemd and
    # from muppet's non-login shells, where super/bin is NOT on PATH (verified
    # 2026-08-12 — muppet has the tool but cannot find it by name).
    import os, shutil
    cands = [os.path.expanduser("~/super/bin/secrets"),
             os.path.expanduser("~/aifabric/bin/secrets"),
             shutil.which("secrets")]
    for exe in cands:
        if not exe or not os.path.exists(exe):
            continue
        try:
            tok = subprocess.run([exe, "get", HA_TOKEN_SECRET],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=45,
                                 check=True).stdout.strip()
        except Exception:
            continue                      # try the next candidate, never raise
        if tok:
            _HA_TOKEN = tok
            return _HA_TOKEN
    print(f"eos-power: cannot read HA token {HA_TOKEN_SECRET} (tried "
          f"{', '.join(c for c in cands if c)})", file=sys.stderr)
    _HA_TOKEN = ""
    return _HA_TOKEN

def _ha_request(path, payload=None, timeout=20):
    """GET (payload None) or POST a JSON request to the HA REST API."""
    import urllib.request, urllib.error
    tok = _ha_token()
    if not tok:
        return None
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        f"{HA_URL}{path}",
        data=json.dumps(payload).encode() if payload is not None else None,
        headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {tok}",
                 "Content-Type": "application/json"},
        method="POST" if payload is not None else "GET")
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:
            body = r.read().decode()
        return json.loads(body) if body.strip() else {}
    except Exception as ex:
        print(f"eos-power: HA request {path} failed: {ex}", file=sys.stderr)
        return None

def zha_state(entity):
    """Current state string of an entity, or None if unreachable."""
    d = _ha_request(f"/api/states/{entity}")
    return d.get("state") if isinstance(d, dict) else None

def zha_power_w():
    """Instantaneous draw in W, or None. SECOND WITNESS ONLY — see module docstring:
    this sensor is delta-driven and does not refresh on demand, so a 0.0 here may
    mean 'no load' or merely 'nothing has reported since the last change'."""
    s = zha_state(ZHA_POWER)
    try:
        return float(s)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None

def _zha_set(powered: bool) -> bool:
    """Switch the ZHA plug and CONFIRM the switch entity actually moved.
    A 200 from HA only means the command was accepted, so re-read the state —
    the same trust-but-verify stance the Dev-number check takes for the rail."""
    want = "on" if (powered == PLUG_ON_POWERS_CAMERA) else "off"
    if _ha_request(f"/api/services/switch/turn_{want}",
                   {"entity_id": ZHA_SWITCH}) is None:
        return False
    for _ in range(6):                      # the S60ZBTPG reports back in ~1-3s
        time.sleep(1)
        if zha_state(ZHA_SWITCH) == want:
            return True
    print(f"eos-power: {ZHA_SWITCH} did not report '{want}' after the command",
          file=sys.stderr)
    return False

async def _plug_command(cmd_name: str) -> bool:
    """Send an On/Off cluster command to the plug over the matter-server WS.
    Auto-discovers the On/Off endpoint from the node's attributes.
    """
    try:
        import websockets
    except ImportError:
        print("eos-power: python 'websockets' module not installed",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    try:
        async with websockets.connect(MATTER_WS, max_size=None,
                                       open_timeout=10) as ws:
            await ws.recv()  # server info banner

            async def call(mid, command, args=None):
                p = {"message_id": str(mid), "command": command}
                if args is not None:
                    p["args"] = args
                await ws.send(json.dumps(p))
                while True:
                    m = json.loads(await asyncio.wait_for(ws.recv(), timeout=60))
                    if m.get("message_id") == str(mid):
                        return m

                # find our plug: by product name if set, else by node id
            nodes = (await call(1, "get_nodes")).get("result", [])

            def has_onoff(n):
                return any(k.endswith(f"/{ONOFF_CLUSTER}/0")
                           for k in n.get("attributes", {}))

            node = None
            if MATTER_VENDOR:
                want = MATTER_VENDOR.strip().lower()
                cands = [n for n in nodes
                         if want in str(n.get("attributes", {}).get("0/40/1",
                                                                    "")).lower()
                         and has_onoff(n)]
                # a reachable match wins over a stale ghost of an old plug
                node = next((n for n in cands if n.get("available")),
                            cands[0] if cands else None)
            if node is None:
                node = next((n for n in nodes
                             if n.get("node_id") == MATTER_NODE), None)
            if node is None:
                print(f"eos-power: no plug found (vendor {MATTER_VENDOR!r}, "
                      f"node {MATTER_NODE}) — not commissioned?",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                return False
            nid = node.get("node_id")
            if not node.get("available"):
                print(f"eos-power: plug node {nid} is commissioned but "
                      f"unavailable (offline?)", file=sys.stderr)
                return False
            ep = next((int(k.split("/")[0])
                       for k in node.get("attributes", {})
                       if k.endswith(f"/{ONOFF_CLUSTER}/0")), None)
            if ep is None:
                print(f"eos-power: node {nid} has no On/Off cluster",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                return False
            r = await call(2, "device_command",
                           {"node_id": nid, "endpoint_id": ep,
                            "cluster_id": ONOFF_CLUSTER,
                            "command_name": cmd_name, "payload": {}})
            if r.get("error_code"):
                print(f"eos-power: plug {cmd_name} failed: {r}", file=sys.stderr)
                return False
            return True
    except Exception as ex:
        print(f"eos-power: matter-server unreachable ({MATTER_WS}): {ex}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False

def _relay_set(powered: bool) -> bool:
    """Switch the plug so the camera is powered (True) or not (False).
    Returns True on success."""
    if not _configured():
        print(f"eos-power: plug not configured ({PLUG_BACKEND} backend) — "
              f"would set camera power {'ON' if powered else 'OFF'}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    if PLUG_BACKEND == "zha":
        return _zha_set(powered)
    cmd = "On" if (powered == PLUG_ON_POWERS_CAMERA) else "Off"
    return asyncio.run(_plug_command(cmd))

# ---- status (best effort, host-independent of the relay) --------------------
def camera_enumerated() -> bool:
    """Is the EOS on muppet's USB bus? (04a9:32e1). Run this ON muppet."""
    return camera_dev() is not None

def camera_dev():
    """The EOS's current (bus, device) numbers, or None if not on the bus.

    This is the GROUND TRUTH for "did the rail actually drop": a power cycle
    makes the camera re-enumerate with a NEW device number. A smart plug that
    ACKs the On/Off command but doesn't actually switch mains leaves the Dev
    number unchanged — indistinguishable from a real wedge without this check.
    (Verified by hand 2026-07-26: Dev 013->014 across a genuine cycle.)"""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["lsusb"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                             timeout=10).stdout
    except Exception:
        return None
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if "04a9:32e1" in line:
            m = re.match(r"Bus (\d+) Device (\d+):", line)
            if m:
                return (m.group(1), m.group(2))
            return ("?", "?")
    return None

def wait_reenumerate(before, timeout=40, poll=2):
    """Wait for the camera to come back with a DIFFERENT device number.

    Returns (ok, dev). ok=False means the rail never dropped (dev unchanged =
    the plug lied) or the camera never came back within `timeout`."""
    deadline = time.time() + timeout
    while time.time() < deadline:
        dev = camera_dev()
        if dev is not None and dev != before:
            return True, dev
        time.sleep(poll)
    return False, camera_dev()

def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("action", choices=["off", "on", "cycle", "status"])
    ap.add_argument("--secs", type=int, default=CYCLE_SECS,
                    help="off-duration for cycle (default ~10s, STATE-validated)")
    ap.add_argument("--no-verify", dest="verify", action="store_false",
                    help="skip the Dev-number check that the rail really dropped")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if args.action == "status":
        up = camera_enumerated()
        print(f"camera {'enumerated' if up else 'NOT on bus'}")
        if PLUG_BACKEND == "zha":
            sw, w = zha_state(ZHA_SWITCH), zha_power_w()
            # The watts are informational ONLY and are ~always 0.0: the EOS is
            # below this meter's floor (see module docstring). 0 W here does NOT
            # mean the camera is off — read the switch state, not the wattage.
            print(f"plug {sw or '?'}"
                  + (f", {w:g} W (below meter floor — not a power indication)"
                     if w is not None else ""))
        sys.exit(0 if up else 1)

    if args.action == "off":
        sys.exit(0 if _relay_set(False) else 2)
    if args.action == "on":
        sys.exit(0 if _relay_set(True) else 2)
    if args.action == "cycle":
        before = camera_dev()
        ok = _relay_set(False)
        if not ok:
            sys.exit(2)
        print(f"eos-power: 12V off, waiting {args.secs}s for rail to drop…",
              flush=True)
        time.sleep(args.secs)
        # NO WATTAGE WITNESS. The EOS draws ~200 mW — BELOW this plug's
        # measurement floor (home-automation, 2026-08-12): ~0.0008 A against a
        # 0.01 A current step, and 1 W power quantisation, i.e. the camera is
        # ~1/12th of one current step. The meter reads 0.0 W with the camera
        # RUNNING NORMALLY, so watts cannot distinguish rail-up from rail-down
        # and would reintroduce the Sandstrom "plug lied" class from the other
        # end (false-pass a cycle that never ran, or false-fail a healthy
        # camera). Earlier bench figures came from a 20-40 W glue gun, not a
        # camera. The USB Dev-number check below remains the ONLY verification.
        # Revisit only if a ~10 W resistive ballast is fitted in parallel on the
        # switched side to lift the load over the passive-reporting floor.
        ok = _relay_set(True) and ok
        print("eos-power: 12V restored", flush=True)
        if not ok:
            sys.exit(2)
        if not args.verify:
            sys.exit(0)
        if before is None:
            print("eos-power: camera was not on the bus before the cycle — "
                  "cannot verify by Dev number", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(0)
        moved, dev = wait_reenumerate(before)
        if moved:
            print(f"eos-power: verified — camera re-enumerated "
                  f"Dev {before[1]} -> {dev[1]} (rail definitely dropped)")
            sys.exit(0)
        if dev == before:
            print(f"eos-power: PLUG LIED — Dev {before[1]} unchanged; the plug "
                  f"ACKed but mains never dropped. Suspect the plug, NOT the "
                  f"camera.", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(3)
        print("eos-power: camera did NOT come back on the bus after the cycle",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(4)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
